Keep the LFS64 wrappers off the real libc names

musl has no separate large-file entry points -- off_t is always 64-bit -- so
under _LARGEFILE64_SOURCE its headers map every *64 name onto the base name:

	fcntl.h:203     #define open64   open
	stdio.h:210     #define fopen64  fopen
	stdio.h:212     #define fseeko64 fseeko
	unistd.h:202    #define lseek64  lseek
	sys/stat.h:102  #define stat64   stat      (and fstat64, lstat64)

The wrappers here are written as `open64(...) { return open(...); }`, so the
macro rewrites both halves and each becomes a call to itself. Worse, the
definition then carries the base name, so linking the archive into an
executable replaces musl's own open, fopen, fseeko, lseek and stat with
infinitely recursive versions. The first call runs off the end of the stack:

	do_page_fault() #2: sending SIGSEGV to rvd for invalid write access
	epc = 77c51284 in rvd[77c2a000+2e000]

the faulting instruction a plain `sw gp,16(sp)` -- a stack store on an
exhausted stack. It fires as soon as anything opens a file, which for rvd is
before it prints a second line, and it is not confined to the shim: every
consumer of the archive loses five libc functions.

`nm musl_shim.o` is the direct check. Before this patch it defines fopen,
open, fseeko, lseek and __stat_time64; after it, the *64 names it means to.

mmap64 escapes today only because <sys/mman.h> is not among the includes --
it is undefined here too so that adding that include later cannot quietly
reintroduce the bug. The deliberate `mmap` override above is untouched.

The T31 libimp needs only base names (`nm -D --undefined-only libimp.so`
lists open, fopen, mmap and stat), so this costs nothing there; the wrappers
exist for the xburst2 blobs, which reference the *64 forms musl does not
otherwise provide, and those keep working.

--- a/musl_shim.c	2026-08-22 13:09:45.608000000 -0700
+++ b/musl_shim.c	2026-08-22 13:09:45.616000000 -0700
@@ -65,6 +65,23 @@
 }
 #endif
 
+/*
+ * musl has no separate large-file entry points, so under _LARGEFILE64_SOURCE
+ * its headers alias every *64 name to the base name. A wrapper written as
+ * open64(...) { return open(...); } then rewrites to a call to itself, and
+ * carries the base name, so it also displaces libc's own implementation for
+ * everything that links this archive.
+ */
+#undef fopen64
+#undef open64
+#undef fseeko64
+#undef ftello64
+#undef lseek64
+#undef mmap64
+#undef stat64
+#undef lstat64
+#undef fstat64
+
 /* Required for Xburst2 libraries */
 
 int __fputc_unlocked(int c, FILE *stream) {
